As said, rotproof cockatrice corpses would be _really_ bad for game
balance.
In general, rotproofing a corpse for food reasons would practically
never be worth a scroll: corpses have very bad nutrition-to-weight
ratios, and if you want the corpse for intrinsics, the requirement for
a tinning kit or enough room in your stomach are deliberate design
decisions; allowing to bypass them would on the whole damage, not
improve gameplay.

> Hmm... perhaps there could be some monsters with attacks that cause
> your food to spoil... or traps...

Bah. That's really too much attention to food for me - and the game
caring first and foremost about messing up a character's inventory is
one of the things i abhor about ADoM (not that inventory damage
matters, but rather that in the late game, losing HP is much less of a
worry than losing gear).

As far as the non-rotting lichen corpses go - most mushrooms contain a
great deal of water and can decay very messily. You need to conserve
them for any type of long-term storage (tinning or drying, usually).
Lichens are quite dry while growing already; getting rid of the
remaining water helps, but they'll last a good while in airy and dry
storage.

